{"causes": ["The Babylonian exile created a context for prophetic voices like Ezekiel to emerge.", "The destruction of Jerusalem in 586 BC heightened the need for messages of hope and restoration among the Jewish people.", "Previous prophetic traditions, such as those of Isaiah and Jeremiah, set a theological framework that Ezekiel expanded upon."], "effects": ["Ezekiel's writings contributed significantly to the development of apocalyptic literature in both Judaism and Christianity.", "His prophecies of restoration influenced Jewish eschatological expectations leading up to and during the Second Temple period.", "Ezekiel's emphasis on individual responsibility and social justice has continued to resonate in modern religious and ethical discussions."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "babylonian-exile", "sourceName": "Babylonian Exile", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "ezekiel", "targetName": "Ezekiel", "context": "Ezekiel's prophetic ministry occurred during the Babylonian Exile, specifically between 593 and 571 BC."}, {"sourceSlug": "ezekiel", "sourceName": "Ezekiel", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "christian-bible", "targetName": "Christian Bible", "context": "Ezekiel's visions and themes significantly influenced the writings and theological frameworks found in the Christian Bible."}, {"sourceSlug": "ezekiel", "sourceName": "Ezekiel", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "prophetic-literature", "targetName": "Prophetic Literature", "context": "Ezekiel's distinctive approach to prophecy contributed to the genre of prophetic literature in both Jewish and Christian traditions."}, {"sourceSlug": "judaism", "sourceName": "Judaism", "verb": "CANONIZES", "targetSlug": "ezekiel", "targetName": "Ezekiel", "context": "Ezekiel is canonized as one of the major prophetic books in the Jewish Tanakh, highlighting his significance in Jewish tradition."}, {"sourceSlug": "ezekiel", "sourceName": "Ezekiel", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "social-justice", "targetName":